1. **Major · new.** Page: “29,000 is far under the establishment survey's bar of about 122,000, so a measured slowdown is more than the release supports.” [BLS’s pinned release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:453) says the 122,000 threshold tests an **over-the-month employment change**. Its table reports August at `133` and September at `29`, in thousands. That threshold tests September’s change against zero, not whether the pace changed significantly from August. The page can say the *reported net gain* was smaller; this statistical test does not establish its conclusion about a slowdown. [Built page](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html).

2. **Major · new.** Page: “employment rose by 406,000 and the labor force by 485,000, both under that survey's bar of about 650,000.” The [pinned BLS FAQ](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:448) gives that approximate bar for a household-survey **employment change**, not every household measure. BLS’s [September significance table](https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cpssigsuma.htm) gives separate exact rows: `Civilian labor force | 170,262 | 485 | 668 | No` and `Employed | 163,152 | 406 | 678 | No`. Neither estimate clears its own bar, but the page applies the employment bar to the labor force.

